  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe cold front is currently moving across coastal plain counties\nheaded towards the coast. Along this cold front, isolated\nshowers are possible, with better coverage rain lagging a couple\nhours behind. Behind this cold front, winds shift to the NW then\nand can gust to 20-30 mph inland. \n\nAs this cold front shifts offshore and stalls along the Gulf \nStream, there is now HIGH confidence that a nor'easter will form\noffshore and bring strong winds, high waves, and coastal \nflooding as well as extremely dangerous marine conditions \nthrough the weekend. There is increasing confidence of impacts \nlingering well into next week as more model guidance is \nsupporting a solution where the nor'easter lingers offshore well\ninto next week.\n\nExactly where the nor'easter tracks after it gets tired of\nsitting offshore and how much it is able to deepen are both\nstill uncertain at this time. It is worth noting that with\naccess to the warm gulf stream waters for multiple days, it is \npossible models are downplaying the eventual strength of the \nlow. \n\nWinds will pick up throughout the day with 25-30 mph gusts \ncommon as the front shifts offshore. Values will increase from \nwest to east with areas near the coast experiencing much higher \nvalues. A wind advisory in effect for Carteret, Hyde, and Dare\ncounties (both OBX and mainland) this afternoon to Monday\nevening, as the High Wind Watch was upgraded to a Wind Advisory\nwith this update. The decision to go with a Wind Advisory \ninstead of a High Wind Warning for OBX boiled down to the OBX \nbeing more resilient when it comes to pure wind impacts, \nespecially considering the nor'easter a couple weeks ago had no \npure wind impacts like trees down or widespread power outages. \nIn addition, probs of wind gusts over 58 mph (HWW criteria) are\ngenerally below 20%. Further extensions into the work week are \nlikely for some if not all zones given model trends. Upgrades \nto High Wind Warnings (especially for OBX) aren't out of the \nquestion if the nor'easter does deepen substantially more than\nmodels suggest, and will be worth monitoring. \n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Impacts are expected to both\nsound and oceanside locales with potentially significant impacts\nto vulnerable areas on the ocean side. A mix of coastal flood\nheadlines is in effect to cover these hazards. Additionally, a \nhigh surf advisory is in effect for the Outer Banks with \nbreaking waves of generally 8-14 feet expected in the surf \nzone. A moderate to high risk of rip currents is also expected \nthrough much of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter record breaking heat the last several days, temperatures\nare expected to cool significantly. Maximum temperatures through\nthe period are expected to remain in the 60-70 degree range with\ncooler values inland and warmer temperatures near the coast.\nOvernight lows will range from the low 40s inland just after the\nfrontal passage to the low 50s by the end of the week with\ncoastal locations in the upper 40s to low 50s early in the\nforecast period to low 50s to low 60s by the end of next week.\nWinds will remain breezy, particularly in the afternoons\ndepending upon the eventual track of the offshore nor'easter.",
